course: biology
lesson: 216
title: Garden birds: robins, blackbirds, tits and feeders
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Start digging in a garden and a robin will often hop down beside your spade within minutes, because every turn of the soil brings up worms and grubs for it.
explain: Long before there were gardeners, robins followed wild boar rooting through the forest floor for exactly the same reason.
explain: That cheerful robin is also one of the fiercest birds in the garden: its red breast is a warning flag, and it will fight other robins to keep its patch, even singing to defend it all through the winter.
explain: A garden is a busy bird town, with blackbirds listening and looking for worms, blue tits hanging upside down, tiny wrens shouting their songs and, at dusk, starlings swirling in clouds of thousands.
fact: Robins follow gardeners because digging brings worms and insects to the surface, and in the wild they follow wild boar for the same reason.
fact: Robins sing all year; both males and females defend territories in winter, and robins sometimes sing at night near street lights.
fact: Robins are fierce defenders of territory; their red breast warns rivals away, and in experiments they attacked even a bunch of red feathers on a stick.
fact: A blackbird tilts its head on the lawn while looking, and maybe listening, for worms; only males are black, while females are brown, which helps them hide on the nest.
fact: Blue tits hang upside down to pick insects from the tips of twigs, and in Britain last century tits learned to open the tops of milk bottles, a trick that spread from bird to bird.
fact: Birds may nest in a nest box in spring, and the size of the hole decides which birds can use it; a small hole keeps out bigger birds that would take the eggs or chicks.
fact: Winter feeding helps small birds survive, because short days leave little time to find food and on a cold night a small bird can lose a noticeable part of its weight keeping warm.
fact: Birds crash into windows because they see reflected trees and sky, and stickers on the glass break up the reflection.
fact: A stunned bird can be put in a box with air holes in a quiet, dark place for a short while and then let go if it recovers.
fact: Fledglings often leave the nest before they can fly well and are still fed by their parents, so a feathered baby bird on the ground is usually best left alone.
fact: A huge swirling cloud of starlings at dusk is called a murmuration; flying together keeps them safer from hawks, and each bird watches only its nearest neighbours.
fact: The tiny wren has one of the loudest songs for its size, which carries through thick bushes.
exercise: Why does a robin follow a gardener who is digging? | Digging brings worms and insects to the surface | It wants to steal the gardener's spade and gloves | It thinks the gardener is its mother | It is guarding the garden for the gardener against cats
exercise: Which wild animal did robins follow before there were gardeners? | Wild boar rooting in the forest | Eagles hunting rabbits in open fields | Brown bears catching fish in rivers | Deer running in big herds across hills
exercise: Why do robins sing in winter? | To defend their winter territories | Because they are cold and shivering | To wake up the spring flowers early | To call for snow
exercise: Which robins defend winter territories? | Both males and females | Only the grown-up adult males | Only the young chicks of the year | No robins, they all fly south
exercise: What did robins attack in experiments? | Even a bunch of red feathers on a stick | Only real hawks that came near the nest | Only their own reflection in water | Nothing, because robins never fight
exercise: What is a robin's red breast for? | Warning rivals away | Keeping its chest warm in the frost | Hiding in red flowers | Showing it has eaten berries
exercise: Why does a blackbird tilt its head on the lawn? | It is looking, and maybe listening, for worms | It is slowly falling asleep in the sun | It has a stiff neck from flying all day long | It is listening for cats only
exercise: What colour is a female blackbird? | Brown | Black | Bright red | Blue
exercise: Why do blue tits hang upside down? | To pick insects from the tips of twigs | To sleep safely, far away from the hungry cats | To see the sky better and spot rain clouds | Because they are dizzy from flying in circles
exercise: What trick did British tits learn last century? | Opening the tops of milk bottles | Opening the doors of parked cars | Ringing doorbells for food | Switching on lights in sheds
exercise: Why does the hole size of a nest box matter? | It decides which birds can use it and keeps out bigger ones | Big holes keep the eggs warmer by letting in the sunshine | Small holes let more rain in | It does not matter at all, birds will use any box
exercise: Why is winter feeding so helpful to small birds? | Short days leave little time to find food and nights are cold | Birds forget how to find food in winter and need reminding | Birds hibernate and need waking up | Insects are easier to find in winter, so seed is a nice treat
exercise: Why do birds fly into windows? | They see reflected trees and sky | They want to come inside where it is warm | They are trying to break the glass on purpose | They are blind in the bright daylight
exercise: How can people help stop birds hitting windows? | Put stickers on the glass to break up the reflection | Wash the windows more often so they shine | Open all the windows all day long, even in winter | Paint the birds a brighter colour
exercise: What should you usually do with a feathered baby bird on the ground? | Leave it, its parents are still feeding it | Take it home and keep it in a shoebox | Put it back up a tree at once, as high as you can | Give it bread and water
exercise: What is a murmuration? | A huge swirling cloud of starlings at dusk | A robin's quiet winter song at night | A wren's nest | A group of ducks paddling together on a pond
exercise: How do starlings in a murmuration avoid crashing? | Each bird watches only its nearest neighbours | One leader tells them all where to go | They fly with their eyes closed and trust luck | They keep far apart from each other
exercise: Why does a tiny wren have such a loud song? | Its song must carry through thick bushes | It is shouting at cats to scare them off | It is deaf | It is copying a robin it heard
test: «3 robins, 2 blackbirds n the cat watching em, how many legs» | 14 | 10 | 20 | 12
test: «4 blue tits on the feeder, 2 flew off, no wait 1 came back. how many wings now» | 6 | 8 | 4 | 3
test: «found a baby bird on the lawn, feathers n hopping, gonna take it home and save it» What is right? | Leave it; it is a fledgling and its parents still feed it | Take it home and feed it milk from a spoon | Put it in the fridge to keep it fresh until it can fly | Throw it up in the air to help it fly
test: «bird just smacked into the window and it's lying there dazed» What should you do? | Put it in a quiet, dark box with air holes, then let it go if it recovers | Shake it gently to wake it up | Give it a warm bath and dry it with a hairdryer straight away | Leave it on the lawn where the cat can reach it, it will sort itself out
test: In Angry Birds, a round red bird gets furious and attacks the green pigs. Which real garden bird is famous for a red breast and fighting? | The robin, which defends its territory fiercely | The wren, which attacks pigs on farms | The starling, which fights hawks alone at dusk | The blue tit, which only fights in summer
test: «my brother reckons the brown blackbird is a different bird» What is right? | It is a female blackbird; only males are black | He is right, it is a song thrush | It is a black one that rolled in mud on the lawn | It is a young robin that has not gone red yet
test: What would happen if a nest box had a very big hole? | Bigger birds could get in and take the eggs | No bird could ever get in or out | The chicks would hatch faster in the fresh air | It would keep the box drier
test: «robin singing outside at midnight by the street lamp, is it mad» What is right? | Robins sometimes sing at night near street lights | Robins only sing at dawn, so it is ill | It is an owl pretending to be a robin to fool you | It is lost and calling for help
test: «why bother feeding birds, they managed for ages without us» Why can it help in winter? | Short days and cold nights make it hard for small birds to get enough food | Birds cannot eat at all in winter | Birds only eat bird seed bought from shops, nothing they find for themselves | Birds hibernate all winter and need food left out to help them wake up
test: «thousands of starlings swirling over the pier, why don't they bump» What is right? | Each bird follows only its nearest neighbours | They are all tied together | A leader shouts directions to the whole flock | They bump all the time and many fall
test: «that cute robin just attacked another robin, what a psycho» Why do robins fight? | They fiercely defend their territory | They are only fighting over one fat worm | They are playing a friendly game of tag | They are cleaning each other's feathers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the tendency of the robin to attend a gardener at work? | Digging exposes worms and insects, as the rooting of wild boar did formerly | The robin regards the gardener as its parent and follows accordingly | The robin is attracted solely by the sound of the spade | The robin wishes to defend the gardener from cats and other enemies of the garden
exam: Identify the principal reason for which birds collide with window glass. | Trees and sky are reflected in the glass and mistaken for open space | Birds desire to enter dwellings in search of warmth and food | Birds are unable to see in daylight | Birds attack the glass in defence of territory against every bird they see
exam: It is given that a feathered young bird is discovered upon the ground, hopping. Ascertain the appropriate course of action. | The bird should ordinarily be left alone, its parents continuing to feed it | The bird should be removed to a dwelling and fed milk until it is grown | The bird should be thrown into the air | The bird should be placed within a refrigerator, so as to preserve it until a veterinarian arrives
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the formation known as a murmuration. | Starlings flock in great numbers at dusk for safety, each bird responding to its nearest neighbours | A single leader directs every bird | It is a song performed by robins in winter beneath the street lamps of towns and cities | It is composed exclusively of wrens, which gather at dusk in order to sing together loudly
